WEBVTT

NOTE
Meeting: Banking Mobile App – Lo-Fi Design Review & Feature Planning
Date: Wednesday, 15 April 2026
Duration: 50:14
Participants:
  Marcus Webb – Product Owner
  Sarah Chen – UX Designer
  Linda Nguyen – Banker / Business Representative
  James Park – Engineer / Tech Lead
  Rachel Torres – Solution Architect

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.420
<v Marcus Webb>Alright, good morning everyone. Let's get started. I'll just do a quick roll call — Sarah, you're on?</v>

2
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.150
<v Sarah Chen>Yep, good morning.</v>

3
00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.600
<v Linda Nguyen>Morning, I'm here.</v>

4
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:11.980
<v James Park>Here. Morning all.</v>

5
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.540
<v Rachel Torres>Morning everyone.</v>

6
00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:28.760
<v Marcus Webb>Perfect. So the purpose of today's session is to do a structured review of the lo-fi designs Sarah put together for the new banking mobile app. We've got two screens to look at — the home dashboard and the account detail screen — and from that we want to walk away with a shared understanding of features, what goes into MVP, and what gets pushed to later releases. I also want us to talk honestly about what we should NOT build, at least not yet. Sound good?</v>

7
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.120
<v Sarah Chen>Sounds great.</v>

8
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.910
<v Linda Nguyen>Agreed, happy to contribute from the business side.</v>

9
00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.440
<v James Park>Yep. I've got a few tech questions already lined up.</v>

10
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:56.300
<v Marcus Webb>Perfect. Sarah, do you want to kick us off with a quick walkthrough of what you've designed? I know everyone received the images in the calendar invite, but let's make sure we're all looking at the same thing and understanding the intent behind each screen before we start pulling it apart.</v>

11
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:32.800
<v Sarah Chen>Sure, happy to. So we've got two screens. The first one is the home screen — the landing page when a customer opens the app. At the top we've got the bank's brand name and some profile and notification icons. Below that is a personalised greeting — "Good Morning, Alex Johnson" — which makes it feel warm and human. Then we have the accounts section. In this lo-fi we've shown two account cards: one is a Savings Account called "Everyday Saver" with a balance of just over twenty-four thousand, and the second is a Mortgage Offset Account called "Home Offset" with a balance of just over sixty-one thousand. Both cards show the BSB, account number, and some relevant product badge — the savings account has the interest rate, the offset account shows "Linked to Mortgage."</v>

12
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:58.200
<v Sarah Chen>Then at the very bottom of the first screen there's a chat assistant prompt — "Ask Nova, your assistant" — with a subtitle that reads "Transfers, statements, loan queries." That's our embedded conversational AI feature. And the bottom navigation bar has four tabs: Home, Pay, Cards, and More. The second screen is the account detail view — so when you tap on the Everyday Saver card, you land here. You see the account name, the available balance, the BSB and account number, and the interest rate badge. Then there are three action buttons: Transfer, Statement, and Export. Below that is a "Recent Transactions" list with a Filter button, showing transactions with the date, description, merchant name, and amount.</v>

13
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:05.440
<v Marcus Webb>Thanks Sarah. Really clear. Linda, before we go into features — does this feel directionally right from a banking product perspective?</v>

14
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:44.600
<v Linda Nguyen>At a high level, yes. I think the layout makes sense. The personalised greeting is something our customers have told us they appreciate — it sounds small but it creates a sense of recognition. The account cards are clean. I do want to flag though that on the first screen, we need to think carefully about the information hierarchy. We're showing balance figures front and centre — and while some customers love that visibility, we've had research that shows some customers, particularly those in financial stress, actually find a big balance number on the home screen anxiety-inducing. But I think we can address that with a show/hide toggle. That's something to factor in.</v>

15
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:47.300
<v Sarah Chen>Oh, that's a really good point. I hadn't thought of it from that angle.</v>

16
00:02:47.300 --> 00:03:05.100
<v Linda Nguyen>Yes, it's something our financial wellbeing team has flagged in the past. The idea being — let the customer control how much they see up front. Some people want to see everything the moment they open the app, others prefer to tap in intentionally. So a toggle to mask balances could be a small but high-value feature.</v>

17
00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:10.400
<v Marcus Webb>Noted. James, Rachel — any initial impressions from a technical standpoint before we dig into specific features?</v>

18
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:44.800
<v James Park>Yeah a few things come to mind. First, it's a clean design and I think from a dev perspective it's very achievable. But I want to flag the Nova chatbot early — that's the one that could balloon in scope really quickly if we're not careful. Depending on how "smart" we want it to be, you're talking about very different levels of complexity. A simple FAQ bot is one thing. A bot that can actually initiate transfers, pull transaction history, or interpret natural language loan queries is a completely different beast. So I want to make sure we scope that very carefully before committing to anything in MVP.</v>

19
00:03:44.800 --> 00:04:12.500
<v Rachel Torres>I'd echo that. And from an architecture standpoint, the two screens themselves are straightforward — standard REST API calls to a core banking system, rendering account data and transactions in a mobile-friendly format. The more interesting architectural questions are around security — specifically around how we handle session management, biometric authentication, and what data we cache locally on the device. The account detail screen with transactions especially — we need to think about pagination, data freshness, and making sure we're not pulling too much data in a single call.</v>

20
00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:17.800
<v Marcus Webb>Good points all around. Let's start going through the features one by one. Sarah, do you want to lead us through what you envisioned for the home screen features first?</v>

21
00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:55.200
<v Sarah Chen>Yes, let's do that. So on the home screen, the core features I'm imagining are: the personalised greeting, account cards with balance and product info, the quick navigation to the account detail, the Nova chatbot entry point, and the bottom nav bar. The account cards are designed to be tappable — so one tap takes you into the detail screen. I also designed them to be horizontally scrollable eventually, assuming a customer has more than two accounts. The Nova chat panel at the bottom is intentionally kept compact on the home screen — it's an entry point, not a full chat interface. The idea is customers can tap it and it expands or opens to a dedicated chat view.</v>

22
00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:24.400
<v Linda Nguyen>Can I jump in on the Nova feature? From the bank's perspective, the value of a chat assistant is enormous — but I want to be very clear about what it should and shouldn't do in the first version. The number one thing our contact centre gets calls about is: "What's my balance?", "Can I see my recent transactions?", "When did this payment come out?" — these are things that are already answered by the app itself if customers know where to look. So Nova's first job should be navigation assistance and answering simple factual questions about account data. It should NOT be making financial recommendations, it should NOT be approving transactions autonomously, and it absolutely should NOT be doing anything that creates a compliance risk.</v>

23
00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:29.800
<v Marcus Webb>Completely agree. We'll come back to the Nova scope. Let's talk about the account detail screen features. Sarah?</v>

24
00:05:29.800 --> 00:06:04.100
<v Sarah Chen>Sure. So on the account detail screen, the main features are: the account header with balance and product info, the three action buttons — Transfer, Statement, and Export — and then the transaction list with Filter. The Transfer button I imagine takes you into a payment flow. Statement would show or generate a formal bank statement. Export would allow the customer to download transactions — probably as a CSV or PDF — for things like tax purposes or expense tracking. The Filter button on the transaction list lets you filter by date range, transaction type, amount range, and maybe merchant category.</v>

25
00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:38.700
<v Linda Nguyen>The Export feature is one I feel strongly about. Our customers — particularly small business customers and anyone doing their own tax — constantly ask for this. Right now they have to log into internet banking, navigate through multiple menus, and download a statement. If we can put that behind one button on the account detail screen, that's a genuine value-add. I'd vote that Export is in MVP, even if it just exports a CSV to start with. PDF can come later. The important thing is that it's there and it works.</v>

26
00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:44.300
<v James Park>CSV is definitely simpler to implement. PDF generation adds a templating layer. I'd agree with Linda — CSV for MVP, PDF in a later sprint.</v>

27
00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:52.100
<v Rachel Torres>We'd also want to consider the security implications of Export. If a customer exports data to their device, we need to make sure that data is handled appropriately — no plain-text sensitive data sitting in a Downloads folder unencrypted.</v>

28
00:06:52.100 --> 00:07:08.400
<v James Park>Good point Rachel. We could handle that by prompting the customer to open the export directly in an associated app rather than saving it locally, or at minimum adding a password protection option on the exported file. That's worth a design decision.</v>

29
00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.900
<v Sarah Chen>I can add that to the design — a confirmation step before export that explains what's being exported and offers some options.</v>

30
00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:38.200
<v Marcus Webb>Good. Let's talk about the transaction list. James, how do we approach that technically? Because depending on how far back we want to show transactions, and whether we're paginating or doing infinite scroll, the complexity changes a lot. I've seen apps that only show thirty days by default and then make you go through Statement for anything older. Is that the right call here?</v>

31
00:07:38.200 --> 00:08:12.600
<v James Park>Yeah, I'd recommend a rolling ninety-day default for the transaction list in the app. Anything beyond that requires a formal statement request. The reason is partly technical — we don't want to load hundreds of transactions on every screen open — and partly UX, because frankly most customers only care about the last few weeks. The Filter feature can let people search within that ninety-day window. But if someone needs six months of data for a home loan application, that's a Statement use case, not a transaction list use case. That distinction also means the Statement feature needs to be quite capable — it needs to be able to generate statements for custom date ranges.</v>

32
00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:18.400
<v Linda Nguyen>Agreed. Our regulatory requirement is that we make twelve months of statements available. So the Statement function must cover at least twelve months, but the live feed can be ninety days.</v>

33
00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:30.100
<v Sarah Chen>That actually makes sense from a UX point of view too. If I'm showing someone five hundred transactions in a scrollable list, that's overwhelming. A ninety-day window with good filtering is a much better experience than a data dump.</v>

34
00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:55.800
<v Marcus Webb>Okay. Let's spend some time now talking about what we shouldn't build — or at least what we should actively defer. I think it's worth naming these things explicitly so we don't get scope creep. Linda, from your side, what are the things that customers ask for but that are actually traps — either too complex, too risky, or not as valuable as they sound?</v>

35
00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:38.200
<v Linda Nguyen>Great question. Number one for me is in-app financial advice. I've been in meetings where people say "let's make the app give personalised budgeting advice" and it sounds fantastic, but the moment you start making recommendations — "you're spending too much on dining" — you're stepping into territory that triggers AFSL obligations in Australia. We're not a financial planner, we're a bank. If we want to offer insights we need to frame them as information, not advice, and that is a very subtle but legally significant distinction. So I'd say: defer anything that looks like advice until we've had legal sign-off and have a proper framework.</v>

36
00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:42.100
<v Marcus Webb>That's a really clear line. James, from a tech perspective, what should we stay away from in the first version?</v>

37
00:09:42.100 --> 00:10:22.400
<v James Park>A few things. First — real-time push notifications for every transaction. I know customers love the idea, but building a reliable, real-time push notification pipeline is non-trivial. You've got to deal with device tokens, notification delivery guarantees, handling cases where the customer has notifications turned off, and the backend needs to be event-driven. If we do it poorly, customers get duplicate notifications, late notifications, or no notifications at all — and that erodes trust fast. So I'd say delayed notifications — maybe batch alerts — are fine for MVP. Real-time notifications, as a proper feature, come in a later release once we've got the infrastructure right. Second — I'd defer any peer-to-peer payment feature like PayID lookup within the app. That's its own integration project.</v>

38
00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.100
<v Marcus Webb>Good flags. Rachel?</v>

39
00:10:24.100 --> 00:11:01.600
<v Rachel Torres>From an architecture and security perspective, I'd say don't build biometric login as an afterthought. I've seen projects that treat Touch ID and Face ID as a "nice to have" and bolt it on at the end, and it creates all sorts of problems — both technically and from a compliance angle. Biometric authentication needs to be designed in from the start as part of the authentication architecture. Even if we don't launch with it on day one, the foundation needs to be there. I'd also flag that we should not build our own cryptography for any sensitive data operations. We use platform-standard libraries. No custom encryption schemes. And on the subject of the mortgage offset account — the "Linked to Mortgage" badge is great, but we should not attempt to show live mortgage balance or redraw calculations in MVP. That's core banking complexity that needs its own integration work.</v>

40
00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:06.400
<v Linda Nguyen>Completely agree on the mortgage piece. Our mortgage system is separate and the integration is not trivial.</v>

41
00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:27.200
<v Sarah Chen>Can I add something from a UX perspective? One thing I'd say we should consciously NOT do is over-animate the interface. There's a trend in fintech apps right now to have lots of micro-animations and transitions — cards flipping, numbers counting up, confetti on transactions — and while it looks impressive in demos, it actually slows down the experience for everyday use. If you open your banking app ten times a day, you do not want to wait for an animation every time. Fast and functional beats flashy.</v>

42
00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:32.800
<v James Park>One hundred percent. And animations are also a significant source of accessibility issues. Keep it clean.</v>

43
00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:58.400
<v Marcus Webb>These are all really valuable. Let me just make sure I'm capturing this: defer real-time push notifications, defer financial advice and insights, defer full mortgage integration, defer PayID and P2P transfers, defer Nova's advanced capabilities, no over-engineering of animations, biometric auth needs to be architecturally considered from the start even if not in MVP launch. That's our "not now" list. Now — let's talk about what customers actually want. Linda, you're closest to the customer voice here.</v>

44
00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:45.800
<v Linda Nguyen>Sure. So the things that come up consistently in our customer research and NPS feedback — and I'll be specific here because I think it'll help us prioritise. Number one is speed. Customers want the app to open quickly and show them their balance without delay. Any more than two seconds to see a balance and you start losing people. Number two is clarity — they want to know exactly what a transaction is. The merchant name is critical. Right now in our legacy app, some transactions show up as cryptic codes and customers call the branch to ask what it is. If we can clean up transaction descriptions and show recognisable merchant names, that's a huge win. Number three is self-service — they want to resolve simple things without calling. Checking balances, viewing transactions, getting a statement — these should all be doable in under thirty seconds without any friction.</v>

45
00:12:45.800 --> 00:13:08.900
<v Sarah Chen>That maps really well to our design. The transaction list already shows the merchant name and category — in the lo-fi you can see "ACME CORP PTY LTD" for the salary, "ANZ ATM GEORGE ST" for the withdrawal, and "AMAZON.COM.AU" for the online purchase. The key will be making sure the data behind those descriptions is clean. That's probably as much a data quality problem as a UX problem.</v>

46
00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:12.500
<v James Park>It is. We'll need a merchant enrichment service. There are third-party providers we can plug into — MCC codes and merchant name normalisation. I'd say that's worth budgeting for even in MVP, because if the transaction descriptions are bad, it doesn't matter how good the UI is.</v>

47
00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:38.200
<v Linda Nguyen>Yes. And the fourth thing customers consistently say is — they want the app to feel like it knows them. Not in a creepy way, but in a "you understand my life" way. The personalised greeting is a start. The fact that we're showing both accounts on the home screen — savings and offset — already creates that sense of a complete financial picture. That's something customers really value. Feeling like the bank sees the whole picture, not just one account.</v>

48
00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:52.400
<v Marcus Webb>And what do customers NOT want? What are the anti-patterns we see from competitor apps that create friction or complaints?</v>

49
00:13:52.400 --> 00:14:35.600
<v Linda Nguyen>Big one: being logged out too frequently. The balance between security timeouts and user experience is a constant battle. If the app logs someone out every five minutes of inactivity, they stop using it. And then they call the branch instead, which costs us money. We need a sensible session policy — something like fifteen minutes of inactivity on a mobile device, with biometric re-authentication to get back in rather than a full password re-entry. Second big anti-pattern: too many steps to do simple things. If it takes four taps to see a transaction detail, that's too many. Every action should be achievable in three taps or fewer from the home screen if possible. Third: jargon. Don't call things "BSB" in the primary label — most customers under thirty don't know what BSB means. We should label it as "Bank Code (BSB)" or something that's human-readable.</v>

50
00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:40.800
<v Sarah Chen>I love that point about jargon. I'll flag that as a copy and content design consideration. We need a content designer or at least a review pass on all labels.</v>

51
00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:55.200
<v Rachel Torres>Related to the session timeout point — from an architecture perspective, I'd propose a refresh token model with a longer lived background session and a shorter lived active session token. That way we can maintain context without keeping a fully active session open. Happy to document a proposed auth flow.</v>

52
00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:58.700
<v Marcus Webb>Yes please Rachel, that would be really helpful as a starting point for the security model.</v>

53
00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:36.400
<v James Park>Can I raise the offline and low-connectivity scenario? Because a lot of banking apps completely fall over when you have poor signal — like on the train or in a building with patchy coverage — and that's actually when customers most urgently need to check their balance. I think we should design for graceful degradation. If the app can't get a live balance, it should show the last known balance with a timestamp — "Balance as of 9:14 AM" — rather than showing a spinner that never resolves or an error screen. Cached data with clear labelling is much better than a failure state.</v>

54
00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:41.500
<v Sarah Chen>Oh that's a great UX consideration. I can design a "cached state" indicator — something subtle so the customer knows they're seeing saved data. That builds trust rather than breaking it.</v>

55
00:15:41.500 --> 00:16:00.400
<v Rachel Torres>From an architecture standpoint, that requires us to define what we cache locally and how long it's valid. I'd suggest balance and last twenty transactions are good candidates for local caching with a thirty-minute TTL. Sensitive things like statement generation should never be served from cache — always a live call.</v>

56
00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:04.100
<v Marcus Webb>Good. Let's now move into the MVP conversation. What goes in, what's the scope. James, can you give us a rough technical read on complexity for each feature?</v>

57
00:16:04.100 --> 00:17:01.200
<v James Park>Sure, I'll go through them. Account dashboard — home screen with personalised greeting, account cards, account balances — that's a medium complexity but foundational. Probably three to four weeks of backend API work and two to three weeks of frontend work running in parallel. The account detail screen — account info, action buttons, transaction list — similar complexity. Transaction data is the main backend piece, and if we're adding merchant enrichment on top, add another two weeks for that integration. Transfer — I'd break this into two parts. Transferring between own accounts is simpler — we can probably do that in MVP. Third-party transfers with BSB and account number verification is more complex. Statement generation — this is actually non-trivial. A properly formatted PDF statement that meets our regulatory requirements needs design templates, data aggregation, and PDF rendering. I'd scope that as a medium-to-large feature. Export as CSV is much simpler — small feature. Nova chatbot — even a basic version is a solid piece of work. Minimum four to six weeks for a useful v1 that's connected to account data.</v>

58
00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:06.800
<v Marcus Webb>So if we're thinking about an MVP that ships in let's say a twelve-week initial build, what would you put in?</v>

59
00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:52.400
<v James Park>I'd say: home screen with account cards and balance, account detail screen with transaction list — rolling ninety days, basic Filter by date range, own-account Transfer, CSV export, and a basic Nova that can answer FAQs and display account data when asked. That's a meaningful product that a customer can actually use for their day-to-day needs. I'd leave Statement generation to release two — it's important but it's not blocking. I'd also leave third-party transfers to release two because of the BSB verification, OSKO/NPP integration considerations. And Nova's advanced features — loan queries, initiated transactions — that's release three or beyond.</v>

60
00:17:52.400 --> 00:18:15.600
<v Linda Nguyen>I'm mostly aligned with that scope. The one thing I'd push back on is Statement. Even if it's not a beautifully formatted PDF, customers need the ability to get a statement in MVP. If we launch without statements and they need one for a loan application, they're going back to the old channel. Can we do a basic version — even just a downloadable CSV or a screen-rendered statement — as a v0.5 of that feature in MVP?</v>

61
00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:24.800
<v James Park>A rendered on-screen view of transactions for a selected date range — like a statement view rather than a downloadable document — that's achievable in MVP. The downloadable PDF version with official formatting, letterhead, digital signatures — that's release two.</v>

62
00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:28.200
<v Linda Nguyen>I can live with that. On-screen statement view in MVP, formatted PDF in release two. That gives customers something useful while we build the proper version.</v>

63
00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:30.500
<v Marcus Webb>Good compromise. Sarah, from a UX design perspective — what needs to be in MVP from a design quality standpoint? And what design elements can be simplified for now?</v>

64
00:18:30.500 --> 00:19:18.400
<v Sarah Chen>The things that absolutely have to be in MVP from a design quality standpoint — the information hierarchy on the home screen, the account card design, and the transaction list legibility. Those are the screens customers will see every day, so they need to be right. I'd also want the balance visibility toggle Linda mentioned to be in MVP — it's not technically complex but it's emotionally impactful. The things I'd simplify for MVP: the onboarding flow — we can use a simple text walkthrough or even skip onboarding if the bank handles account setup separately. Accessibility optimisation — we need to meet baseline WCAG 2.1 AA standards, but deep accessibility refinement like voice navigation optimisation can be post-MVP. Dark mode — I'd design for it architecturally but not launch with it in MVP. And the Filter UI — we can start with a simple date range picker and expand to full filtering in release two.</v>

65
00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:22.400
<v Marcus Webb>Rachel, from an architecture perspective — what absolutely must be in the foundational layer even if users don't see it?</v>

66
00:19:22.400 --> 00:20:02.600
<v Rachel Torres>The non-negotiables from an architecture standpoint are: secure API gateway with proper auth — I'd propose OAuth 2.0 with PKCE for the mobile client. Certificate pinning on the mobile app to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks. Data encryption at rest and in transit — nothing negotiable there. Audit logging — every action a customer takes within the app needs to be logged for compliance and fraud investigation purposes. Rate limiting on API calls to prevent abuse. And a feature flag system from day one — this is critical for a product that's going to evolve quickly. We need to be able to turn features on and off per customer segment without a full app release. That's how we do safe rollouts and A/B testing down the line.</v>

67
00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:06.100
<v Marcus Webb>Feature flags — yes, one hundred percent. James, that's agreed?</v>

68
00:20:06.100 --> 00:20:10.400
<v James Park>Absolutely. We'll use a feature flag service — I'd recommend LaunchDarkly or an equivalent. It pays for itself immediately.</v>

69
00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:34.200
<v Marcus Webb>Good. Let's talk about the Nova chatbot a bit more because I think it's one of the most differentiating features in the design, but also the one with the most risk if we get it wrong. Linda, what's the business case for including it in MVP versus deferring it to a later release?</v>

70
00:20:34.200 --> 00:21:18.400
<v Linda Nguyen>The business case for Nova in MVP is primarily contact centre deflection. Every call that Nova handles is a call we don't pay an agent to handle. Our average cost per contact centre call is around seven to ten dollars. If Nova can deflect even ten percent of calls from customers who just want to check a balance or understand a transaction — and let's say we have fifty thousand active customers — the math becomes compelling very quickly. The other case is availability. Our contact centre operates nine to five. Nova is available twenty-four seven. For a customer checking their account at eleven PM on a Sunday, Nova is the only option. That has real value. But — and this is important — Nova has to be good. A bad chatbot is worse than no chatbot. If it frustrates customers, they lose trust in the whole app.</v>

71
00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:20.300
<v James Park>Agreed. A chatbot that says "I didn't understand that" to every second question will be the most complained-about feature.</v>

72
00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:55.600
<v Sarah Chen>From a UX standpoint, I designed Nova as a non-intrusive entry point on the home screen — it's there for those who want it, but it doesn't get in the way of the main account view. The key design principle I'd advocate for is: Nova should always have a clear escalation path. If it can't help, it should say so clearly and offer to connect the customer to a human. "I can help with account questions. For anything more complex, I can connect you to our team." That kind of graceful handoff is what separates a good bot experience from a frustrating one. The worst thing we can do is trap a customer in a bot loop when they need a human.</v>

73
00:21:55.600 --> 00:22:38.200
<v Rachel Torres>Technically, Nova's architecture is its own decision point. We can go with a fully proprietary LLM-backed conversational AI, which gives us maximum control but maximum cost and complexity. Or we can use a platform like Dialogflow, Microsoft Bot Framework, or even a banking-specific conversational AI vendor. For MVP I'd recommend a platform-based approach — you get natural language understanding out of the box, you can build intents for the specific queries we care about, and you avoid having to build infrastructure from scratch. The integration into our core banking APIs is the bespoke work. The NLU layer should be bought, not built. Then in a later release, if we want to swap in a more sophisticated model, we can do that behind the same interface.</v>

74
00:22:38.200 --> 00:22:45.500
<v Marcus Webb>That sounds pragmatic. So for MVP, Nova is a platform-based chatbot with a defined set of intents — what would those be?</v>

75
00:22:45.500 --> 00:23:28.100
<v Linda Nguyen>I'd say the minimum viable intents for Nova are: check balance, view recent transactions, understand a specific transaction — "what was this charge?" — request a statement, and how to make a transfer. Those five intents cover the vast majority of what customers call us about. We can also add some general FAQ intents — branch hours, product information, contact details — those are straightforward and help deflect calls. What I'd specifically keep OUT of Nova's scope in MVP is anything that involves transactional actions — so Nova should not be able to initiate a transfer, it should not be able to change account settings, and it should not be able to do anything that has financial or legal implications. It can surface information and guide customers to the right place in the app. That's it.</v>

76
00:23:28.100 --> 00:23:35.400
<v James Park>That's a clean boundary. Read-only Nova for MVP. Write operations come in a later release once we've tested the trust model.</v>

77
00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:40.200
<v Sarah Chen>And we name it appropriately in the design — "Ask Nova" implies asking, not instructing. The language already sets that expectation.</v>

78
00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:56.800
<v Marcus Webb>Good. I want to spend a few minutes on the Mortgage Offset Account specifically. The lo-fi shows it on the home screen with a "Linked to Mortgage" badge. What does that mean in terms of functionality, and what can we realistically build?</v>

79
00:23:56.800 --> 00:24:45.200
<v Linda Nguyen>An offset account is a transaction account linked to a mortgage, where the balance in the offset account reduces the amount of interest charged on the mortgage. So if your mortgage is five hundred thousand and your offset is sixty-one thousand, you only pay interest on four hundred thirty-nine thousand. Customers care deeply about this — they track their offset balance vigilantly because they understand every dollar there saves them money. What they want to see in the app is: the offset balance, which loan it's linked to, and ideally some indication of how much interest they're saving. The "Linked to Mortgage" badge in the design is a start. Ideally, tapping that badge or the account card should give the customer some context about the linkage.</v>

80
00:24:45.200 --> 00:25:22.600
<v Rachel Torres>The challenge is that mortgage system data lives in a different core system — our loan origination and servicing platform — which is not the same system that houses the transaction accounts. So showing real-time "interest saved" calculations requires a cross-system query and some fairly complex business logic. For MVP, I'd recommend we show: offset account balance, the account number and BSB, and the "Linked to Mortgage" indicator. We do NOT attempt to show the linked mortgage balance or interest savings calculations. A tap on the badge can show a static explanation — "This account is linked to your home loan. Your offset balance reduces the interest you pay." That gives customers context without requiring the complex integration.</v>

81
00:25:22.600 --> 00:25:27.400
<v Linda Nguyen>I'm okay with that for MVP. The interest savings calculation is on our wishlist for release two — it's a big feature and customers love it, but it can wait.</v>

82
00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:32.100
<v Marcus Webb>Great. Let me now try to synthesise what we've discussed into a high-level release roadmap. I'll name the features and you tell me if I've got it right. MVP — Release One?</v>

83
00:25:32.100 --> 00:26:15.800
<v Marcus Webb>Home screen with personalised greeting, account cards showing savings and offset accounts, balance visibility toggle, bottom navigation — Home, Pay, Cards, More. Account detail screen with transaction list — ninety-day rolling window, basic date-range filter, merchant name enrichment. Three action buttons: Transfer — own accounts only, On-screen statement view — filtered date range, CSV export. Nova chatbot entry point — five core intents, read-only, platform-based NLU. Offset account — balance display and linked badge with static explanation. Foundational architecture — OAuth 2.0 PKCE, certificate pinning, encryption, audit logging, feature flags. Dark mode design-ready but not shipped.</v>

84
00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:22.900
<v James Park>That's a strong MVP. I'd want to stress-test the timeline but the scope feels right. I'd say twelve to sixteen weeks with a team of three frontend engineers, two backend engineers, one QA and Rachel on arch.</v>

85
00:26:22.900 --> 00:26:26.200
<v Linda Nguyen>And a content designer — I really want that on the team even if part-time. The copy in a banking app matters enormously.</v>

86
00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:28.100
<v Sarah Chen>A hundred percent — I'll flag that as a resourcing need.</v>

87
00:26:28.100 --> 00:26:42.300
<v Marcus Webb>Agreed. Content designer goes on the team list. Now Release Two — what are the obvious candidates?</v>

88
00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:58.400
<v Linda Nguyen>Formatted PDF statement download, third-party transfers with BSB and account number, interest savings display for offset accounts, transaction search — keyword search within the transaction list, and push notifications — delayed batch alerts to start.</v>

89
00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:18.600
<v James Park>I'd add: full transaction filter — by category, amount range, merchant — biometric login going live if not in MVP, dark mode launch, and potentially the beginning of open banking integration if we're going down that path. Also I'd put the PayID integration in release two — it's a table-stakes feature for a modern banking app but it's a proper integration project.</v>

90
00:27:18.600 --> 00:27:36.400
<v Rachel Torres>From an architecture perspective, release two is also when I'd want to introduce a proper API versioning strategy, if it's not already in place, and a more sophisticated caching layer — especially if we're adding more account types or doing open banking. Also consider a native analytics layer — tracking in-app behaviour so we can make data-driven decisions about what to build in release three.</v>

91
00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:52.400
<v Sarah Chen>Design-wise for release two: the full filter UI which I kept simple in MVP, onboarding — a proper interactive first-run experience for new customers, and I'd want to revisit the account detail screen to add a spending insights panel — a simple breakdown of categories. Not advice, just data.</v>

92
00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:55.800
<v Linda Nguyen>Spending categories — yes, as long as legal are comfortable with the framing. Information not advice.</v>

93
00:27:55.800 --> 00:28:08.400
<v Marcus Webb>Good. Release Three — what's the longer horizon?</v>

94
00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:52.200
<v Linda Nguyen>Release three is where I'd put the more ambitious features. Nova with transactional capability — so it can actually initiate a transfer on your behalf after strong authentication. Goal-based savings features — being able to create a savings goal and track progress. Loan application flows — so a customer can start a personal loan or credit card application from within the app. Round-up and micro-savings features — rounding up purchases and saving the difference. And integration with open banking data to show accounts from other banks. That's the full financial picture play. That's when the app stops being a "check your balance" tool and starts being a genuine financial management platform.</v>

95
00:28:52.200 --> 00:29:04.200
<v James Park>I'd add to release three: real-time notifications infrastructure done properly, watch app integration, and potentially a widget for the home screen — so customers can see their balance without even opening the app.</v>

96
00:29:04.200 --> 00:29:22.800
<v Rachel Torres>And from an architecture standpoint, release three is when you'd want to have a proper event-driven architecture in place — because features like real-time notifications and Nova initiating transactions require that backbone. MVP can be request-response APIs. But at scale, with those advanced features, you need event streaming.</v>

97
00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:40.100
<v Marcus Webb>This is all really helpful. Let me take a step back and ask — is there anything in the lo-fi design that we've looked at today that you think is wrong or misleading? Any design decision that we should challenge before we go further?</v>

98
00:29:40.100 --> 00:30:12.600
<v Sarah Chen>I'll challenge my own design — the three-button layout on the account detail screen: Transfer, Statement, Export. In user testing on a real device, three equal-weight buttons in a row can create a decision paralysis moment. I might suggest we reconsider making Transfer more visually prominent since it's the most common action, and grouping Statement and Export under a secondary "More options" or "Manage" button. That way the most used action is the clearest call to action, and the less frequently used ones are one tap away but not competing for equal attention.</v>

99
00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:18.100
<v James Park>That makes sense. And from a tech standpoint, a "More options" button pattern is also easier to extend — you can add more actions behind it in future releases without redesigning the header layout every time.</v>

100
00:30:18.100 --> 00:30:22.600
<v Linda Nguyen>I like that. Transfer as primary, everything else secondary. Customers use transfer probably ninety percent more than they request statements.</v>

101
00:30:22.600 --> 00:30:50.400
<v Rachel Torres>One thing I'd flag from the architecture side looking at the home screen design — the account cards currently show the full account number. That's a security concern. In many modern banking apps, the account number is partially masked by default — showing only the last four digits — with the option to reveal it via a tap and authentication. We should not be displaying full account numbers in plain text on the home screen. That's a risk even if the device is just visible to a third party for a moment.</v>

102
00:30:50.400 --> 00:30:54.200
<v Sarah Chen>Absolutely, I'll update the design to show masked account numbers by default. The lo-fi was just for illustration but you're right — we should bake that in from the start.</v>

103
00:30:54.200 --> 00:31:06.600
<v Linda Nguyen>Agreed. From a regulatory standpoint, displaying full account numbers without authentication to reveal them would be something our security team would flag immediately in a design review. Good catch Rachel.</v>

104
00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:22.400
<v Marcus Webb>Brilliant. Last topic before we wrap — the bottom navigation bar. Four tabs: Home, Pay, Cards, More. Does everyone agree with this navigation structure? Are there things that should be there that aren't, or things that don't belong?</v>

105
00:31:22.400 --> 00:31:58.600
<v Sarah Chen>The four-tab structure is a deliberate choice based on what research shows customers do most. Home is the dashboard — that's where you start. Pay covers all payment-related flows. Cards is for debit and credit card management — controlling card limits, freezing a card, managing card settings. And More is the catch-all for everything else — profile, settings, notifications, help, legal documents. The reason I kept it to four tabs is that research consistently shows that five tabs is the point where customers start losing track. Four tabs with a "More" overflow is the sweet spot for discoverability versus simplicity.</v>

106
00:31:58.600 --> 00:32:14.200
<v Linda Nguyen>I'd want to validate that "Pay" covers both internal transfers and external bill payments. Because bill payment — paying utilities, insurance, council rates — is something customers do very regularly and it needs to be clearly accessible. Is that within "Pay" scope?</v>

107
00:32:14.200 --> 00:32:19.400
<v Sarah Chen>Yes, absolutely. Pay would cover: transfer between own accounts, transfer to saved payees, BPAY bill payment, and new payee setup. All within that one tab.</v>

